* ACC_OS_AKPATH
* Set a restored hashed file's relocated index path.
* Copyright (c) String Database
*
* START-HISTORY:
* 01 Oct 26 dm Written for S.50 (per-OS helper; agreed with SD Core for Windows
*              by mail, 2026-10-01T1115 and 1200).
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call !acc_os_akpath(data.path, ak.path, err)
*
*    Runs only after acc_os_place: sdidx -p refuses a directory that does not
*    exist yet, so it cannot run on the staged tree.  On Linux the placed tree
*    belongs to the account's own user, so it goes through root -
*    "sd-elevate akpath-set", which keeps both paths inside one account and
*    accepts the change only when sdidx -q then names the new path (sdidx -p
*    itself prints nothing and ignores its own write's result).
*
*    SUCCESS IS THE HELPER'S "index path of <data> is now <ak>" LINE.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine acc_os_akpath(data.path, ak.path, err)
$catalog !acc_os_akpath

$include int$keys.h
$include accos.h

   err = ''
   if index(data.path:ak.path, "'", 1) then
      err = 11013:@vm:data.path
      return
   end

   out = ''
   os.execute ACCOS$ELEVATE:" akpath-set '":data.path:"' '":ak.path:"' 2>&1" capturing out
   stat = os.error()
   want = 'index path of ':data.path:' is now ':ak.path
   ok = @false
   n = dcount(out, @fm)
   for i = 1 to n
      if index(out<i>, want, 1) then ok = @true
   next i
   if stat # 0 or not(ok) then
      err = 11019:@vm:data.path:@vm:stat:@vm:convert(@fm, '|', out) ;* Setting the index path failed
   end
   return

end

* END-CODE
